<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Arezzo</h1><p class="st-meta">1098 CE – 1384 CE (approx.) · capital Arezzo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ghibelline city-republic of eastern Tuscany whose defeat at Campaldino in 1289 began its long decline before the power of Florence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, an old Etruscan and Roman town, was dominated by its powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, but consuls appear by 1098. The commune grew in the 12th century, contesting control of the town and the rural castles with the bishops and with neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-siena">Siena</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governed by consuls, then a podestà and later priors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> was generally Ghibelline. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> remained political leaders: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmino-degli-ubertini">Guglielmino degli Ubertini</a> died fighting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-campaldino">Campaldino</a> in 1289, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guido-tarlati">Guido Tarlati</a>, bishop from 1312 and lord of the city from 1321 until his death in 1327, expanded the city's territory and rebuilt its walls. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> was born in the city in 1304, his family having been exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guido-tarlati">Tarlati</a>'s death the city fell under outside lords and was racked by faction. In 1384 the French captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enguerrand-vii-de-coucy">Enguerrand de Coucy</a> sold it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence</a>, which ruled it for the rest of its history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1098 CE – 1384 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.55%;width:5.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1098 CE – 1384 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arezzo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guglielmino degli Ubertini, Guido Tarlati</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuscan Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arezzo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1241&amp;focus=commune-of-arezzo" aria-label="Commune of Arezzo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4966 636 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Arezzo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5286 892L5229 694M5286 892L5270 883"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arezzo. <a href="/atlas?year=1241&amp;focus=commune-of-arezzo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

